window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-08d17d9ce0e9eb69"]={"key":"ny_c-08d17d9ce0e9eb69","group":"ny_c","uid":"08d17d9ce0e9eb69","rank":79,"title":"cravenhurst ent records root cause briefing","occupation":"medical_secretaries_admin_assistants","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c2","prompt":"You are Marta Kilbride, the medical secretary at Cravenhurst ENT & Audiology, preparing a briefing for Yusra Behrmann, our clinical operations manager. About one in five new patients arrives without the outside imaging or audiogram the visit depends on, so the appointment is often burned at check-in; Yusra wants a defensible answer before the fifth provider is added in October. We have only traces rather than a clean dataset—fax confirmations, Zendesk tickets, and scheduling notes—and there are four plausible stories in circulation: the referral fax form has no records field, the scheduling script asks for imaging but not the report, the reminder call dropped that line during a spring template edit, and the intake fax number changed without referring offices being told. Prepare a concise executive root-cause briefing memo that teaches a senior reader how the evidence supports the conclusion, projects the likely operational effect before October under clearly stated assumptions or scenarios, and makes exactly one recommended change. Yusra has already informally promised a high-volume referring office that its referrals will not be rejected or delayed for missing records, although our professional obligations still require us to avoid implying that an incomplete record is safe or ready for clinical use; a rare case involving a potentially urgent finding in outside material is severe enough that it must not disappear inside ordinary missing-record guidance. Explain how these tensions affect the proposed fix without inventing patient facts, measured rates beyond the brief, legal requirements, or savings. Distinguish observed information from assumptions, identify what evidence would confirm or weaken the leading explanation, and make the recommendation specific enough for an executive to understand its owner, timing, control point, and success measure without turning the memo into system click instructions. Use plain, privacy-conscious language and do not include patient identifiers. Write the finished memo to `cravenhurst_records_root_cause_briefing.docx` in the top level of the working directory, keep it to no more than three pages, and end on the single decision rather than a list of future tasks.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `cravenhurst_records_root_cause_briefing.docx` — A concise executive root-cause briefing memo explaining the missing outside-records problem, weighing competing explanations, projecting implications before October, and making one defensible recommended change. (The file must be written for Yusra Behrmann as an executive or senior decision maker.; The memo must be no more than three pages.; The memo must make exactly one recommendation and end with that decision.; Observed facts, assumptions, projections, and unknowns must be distinguishable.; Do not include patient identifiers, invented patient-level facts, invented measured rates, unsupported legal claims, or system click instructions.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/cravenhurst_records_root_cause_briefing.docx","bytes":41457,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/a55d1dcf72602a12947a958a375c4350d841df62f2fd0b9a91d4689c17e2b87d.docx","sha256":"a55d1dcf72602a12947a958a375c4350d841df62f2fd0b9a91d4689c17e2b87d","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"CRAVENHURST ENT & AUDIOLOGY\nExecutive root-cause briefing | Outside imaging and audiogram readiness\nTO\nYusra Behrmann, Clinical Operations Manager\nFROM\nMarta Kilbride, Medical Secretary\n\nDECISION IN BRIEF\nAbout one in five new patients arrives without the outside imaging or audiogram material the visit depends on; the appointment can be burned at check-in instead of used for care. The traces available—fax confirmations, Zendesk tickets, and scheduling notes—support a process-control failure, but do not prove one cause. The most actionable lead is a request-design gap: the referral form and call script do not consistently name both the study and its associated report. A single intake control that makes both components explicit, verifies receipt before the visit, and routes exceptions to an owner should protect capacity ahead of October without claiming that every missing record will disappear.\nWHAT IS KNOWN, ASSUMED, AND STILL UNKNOWN\nSTATUS\nWHAT IT MEANS HERE\nBOUNDARY\nOBSERVED\nBrief reports roughly 1 in 5 new patients lacks required outside imaging or an audiogram; check-in may consume the appointment. Evidence is limited to fax confirmations, Zendesk tickets, and scheduling notes.\nNo clean denominator, date range, cause count, or patient-level facts were supplied.\nASSUMPTION FOR PROJECTION\nThe current mix and arrival pattern remain broadly similar through September; “ready” means the correct study and report are received, legible, matched to the record, and available to the clinician.\nThis is a planning scenario, not a measured forecast.\nUNKNOWN\nWhether the gap is concentrated in a few referring offices; whether the spring edit changed frequency; whether faxes reached the current number; and how often an image arrived without its report.\nThese unknowns limit causal certainty and require trace-level checks.\nEVIDENCE AND COMPETING EXPLANATIONS\nThe traces should be read as a chronology and coverage test, not as a causal dataset. A repeated pattern across later tickets and notes would strengthen a persistent intake-design problem; a sharp change immediately after a template version would instead point to the reminder. A fax confirmation proves an attempt or transmission record, not that a usable, correctly matched document reached the chart.\nHYPOTHESIS\nMECHANISM / WHAT TO LOOK FOR\nCURRENT WEIGHT\nReferral fax form has no records field\nAn upstream omission: referring staff may send the referral but not know which outside material is required. Compare form versions and ticket language; check whether missing-record cases recur across offices using the same form.\nPlausible, broad coverage; explains request failure, not a confirmed routing loss.\nScript asks for imaging, not the report\nDistinct content failure: an image and its radiology/audiology report are not interchangeable. Look for notes saying “imaging received” while the report is absent, and compare call wording with later tickets.\nStrong actionable lead if that pattern repeats.\nSpring reminder-template edit dropped the line\nTime-bounded explanation. Align the template-change date with the first sustained rise in tickets/notes and compare pre/post wording.\nCould explain a step-change; weak if missing records predate the edit or persist unchanged.\nIntake fax number changed without notice\nTransmission/routing failure affecting offices differently from internal scripts. Match confirmations and failures to old/new destinations and test whether a sender’s confirmation lacks a corresponding receipt.\nStrong only where routing evidence clusters; does not explain a complete report being omitted after successful receipt.\nLeading explanation (bounded): the missing-report/request-language gap is the best operational lead, possibly amplified by the spring reminder edit. It fits the distinction between transmission and content failure and can persist across repeated notes. It remains an inference: a clean comparison of form/script versions, timestamps, sender office, destination number, and received components could confirm it. It would be weakened if most cases show no delivery to the new fax number, or if reports are routinely requested and still absent despite verified routing.\nIMPLICATION BEFORE OCTOBER (CONDITIONAL)\nIf the assumption above holds, leaving the pathway unchanged means the same approximate one-in-five exposure will meet a larger provider schedule: more check-in rework, avoidable delays, and less usable appointment capacity. If the spring edit or a routing defect is the dominant mechanism, the effect will be concentrated after its change date or among particular offices; the proposed control still detects the missing component early, but will not by itself repair an external number or guarantee response. No exact visit count, savings, or provider-capacity claim is supportable from the supplied traces.\nONE RECOMMENDED CHANGE: A PRE-VISIT RECORDS-READINESS GATE\nApprove one accountable change: the Clinical Operations Manager (Yusra Behrmann) owns a standard “records readiness” gate in the intake pathway, live before the October provider expansion. The gate uses one plain-language request to internal schedulers and referring offices: identify the required study (imaging or audiogram) and the associated report, send through the verified intake route, and document source plus receipt date/time. A records coordinator (or named scheduling delegate) checks the gate before the appointment is protected for provider use; status is recorded as ready, missing, or unusable.\n“Ready” is more than “fax sent”: verify minimum-necessary patient matching (for example, name and date of birth or medical-record number), legibility, correct source, and that both image/study and report are present when required. An unreadable, mismatched, or incomplete item is marked unusable, assigned to the records coordinator for a documented recovery request, and kept attached to the appointment rather than silently discarded. This gives referring offices a respectful path to supply material and preserves Yusra’s service promise: referrals are not rejected for missing records, but incomplete material is never represented as clinically ready.\nA potentially urgent finding in outside material is a separate safety exception. Administrative staff must not interpret the image or decide urgency; they should flag the concern and route it promptly to the responsible clinician or established clinical escalation owner. Routine missing-record reminders must never delay that review.\nMeasure success as a before/after trend: among new-patient appointments requiring outside material, track the share marked ready (both components, matched and usable) by the defined pre-visit checkpoint, using the same definition for a baseline period before the gate and each period after launch. Review alongside repeat requests, delayed or unused appointments, and office-level patterns so an apparent improvement does not hide extra burden or delayed care. This target is a proposed management measure, not an observed rate. Residual risks include nonresponse, wrong routing, and clinically important material arriving too late; the gate cannot eliminate them.\nDECISION\nYusra should authorize the single pre-visit records-readiness gate, with herself as accountable owner and the records coordinator as operational owner, for launch before October. This is the decision: make required study plus report, verified receipt, and safe exception routing the one standard intake control.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A readable DOCX file named cravenhurst_records_root_cause_briefing.docx is present.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Submitted working-directory file.","grader_hint":"Verify the exact filename, DOCX validity, and that the document opens."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered memo is no more than three pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX with a standard offline office renderer and count pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies Cravenhurst ENT & Audiology as the setting.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening or context text.","grader_hint":"Search the rendered text for the organisation name."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo is addressed to or explicitly frames its audience as Yusra Behrmann, clinical operations manager.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience line or opening paragraph.","grader_hint":"Confirm both the named recipient and decision-making role."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo states that roughly one in five new patients arrives without required outside imaging or audiogram material.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Problem statement.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording that preserves the approximate rate and population."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo connects missing outside records to appointments being unusable or burned at check-in, rather than treating the issue as mere paperwork inconvenience.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Problem-impact explanation.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit operational consequence at check-in or during the visit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo links the decision horizon to the planned fifth provider in October.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing statement.","grader_hint":"Verify that October and provider expansion are connected."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo names fax confirmations, Zendesk tickets, and scheduling notes as the available evidence traces.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence inventory.","grader_hint":"All three trace types must appear or be clearly paraphrased."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo distinguishes the four plausible explanations rather than collapsing them into one generic process failure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cause analysis.","grader_hint":"Confirm separate treatment of the form, scheduling script, reminder template, and changed fax-number explanations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The referral fax form's lack of a records field is described as a possible upstream omission mechanism.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Hypothesis discussion.","grader_hint":"The memo must explain how the omission could cause records not to arrive."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The scheduling script's request for imaging but not the report is described as a distinct failure mode.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Hypothesis discussion.","grader_hint":"Require recognition that an image and its report are not interchangeable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The spring reminder-template edit is treated as a time-bounded change that could explain a shift in missing-record frequency.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Temporal analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit temporal reasoning, not just repetition of the hypothesis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The changed intake fax number is treated as a transmission or routing failure that may affect referring offices differently from internal scripts.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Hypothesis discussion.","grader_hint":"The explanation should identify the external-routing mechanism."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo evaluates hypotheses against timing, source coverage, recurrence, or corroboration instead of choosing a cause solely because it sounds plausible.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparative analysis.","grader_hint":"Require at least two explicit comparison dimensions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies one leading explanation or one clearly bounded causal combination, rather than presenting four co-equal causes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conclusion.","grader_hint":"A ranked conclusion is acceptable only if one lead is unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The leading explanation is supported by later or repeated evidence as requested, while the memo acknowledges that the traces do not prove causation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-to-conclusion reasoning.","grader_hint":"Look for both supporting persistence and an appropriately qualified evidentiary claim."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo states what evidence would confirm the leading explanation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Validation statement.","grader_hint":"Accept concrete record comparisons, timestamps, or routing checks tied to the selected cause."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo states what evidence would weaken or falsify the leading explanation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Validation statement.","grader_hint":"The proposed disconfirming evidence must be specific and relevant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo separates information given in the brief from assumptions made for projection.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, table, or explicit prose.","grader_hint":"A reader must be able to tell which claims are supplied facts and which are assumed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies important unknowns caused by the absence of a clean dataset.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations or uncertainty text.","grader_hint":"Examples include denominator, time period, referral-office distribution, or cause-specific counts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any projected outcome before October is explicitly conditional on stated assumptions or scenarios.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Projection section.","grader_hint":"Reject an unqualified forecast stated as an observed result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The projection addresses appointment capacity, rework, delays, or another decision-relevant operational outcome.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Projection text.","grader_hint":"The outcome must matter to the October expansion decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo does not convert the one-in-five brief figure into unsupported precise counts, savings, or provider-capacity claims.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quantitative statements.","grader_hint":"Check that no invented volume, dollar value, percentage change, or exact forecast is presented as fact."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo explains why the proposed change is expected to address the selected mechanism, not merely the symptom.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"Trace the change from causal mechanism to earlier record availability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo makes exactly one primary recommended change.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive recommendation.","grader_hint":"Do not count supporting safeguards or measurement as separate recommendations; reject multiple alternative calls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommended change is operationally specific enough to identify what should be changed in the patient-records intake pathway.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation wording.","grader_hint":"A vague call to improve communication is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation identifies an accountable owner or role.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation or implementation boundary.","grader_hint":"Accept a named person or clearly defined operational role."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation identifies when it should be in place relative to the October provider expansion.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing statement.","grader_hint":"A relative deadline such as before October is acceptable if unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation identifies a control point where record completeness or routing can be checked before the appointment is lost.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Control description.","grader_hint":"The control must occur before or early enough to protect appointment use."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo proposes a measurable success indicator tied to required outside records arriving in time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Success measure.","grader_hint":"Accept a rate, count, completeness measure, or trend with a defined numerator and denominator concept."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The success measure has a comparison period, baseline, or before-and-after logic.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Measurement definition.","grader_hint":"The measure must support judging whether the change worked."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo distinguishes an imaging study from the associated report when defining record completeness.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Completeness definition.","grader_hint":"Require explicit recognition of both components where clinically relevant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo treats records as needing to be available for clinical use, not merely sent or logged as transmitted.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational definition.","grader_hint":"Look for receipt, legibility, correct patient matching, or clinician availability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo includes a way to handle a record that is received but unreadable, mismatched, incomplete, or otherwise unusable.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception or quality-control language.","grader_hint":"Any concrete unusable-record disposition is acceptable if it preserves safety."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo preserves a route for the referring office or patient to supply missing material without treating a missing record as a reason to silently discard the appointment.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational handling.","grader_hint":"Look for a documented recovery or escalation path."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo addresses the informal promise to the high-volume referring office explicitly.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tension discussion.","grader_hint":"The promise must be acknowledged rather than ignored."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo does not interpret the informal promise as permission to represent incomplete records as complete or clinically ready.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Promise-handling language.","grader_hint":"Require a clear boundary between service accommodation and unsafe assurance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo offers a professional communication approach that preserves the relationship while accurately describing record requirements.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stakeholder communication.","grader_hint":"The language should be respectful and factual, not punitive or misleading."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies the rare severe exception involving potentially urgent findings in outside material.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception treatment.","grader_hint":"The severe exception must be recognizable as distinct from routine missing paperwork."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo gives the severe exception a separate escalation or clinical-review path rather than folding it into routine reminder or scheduling guidance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception treatment.","grader_hint":"A generic 'follow up' statement is insufficient; require prompt clinical ownership or escalation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo does not diagnose the patient, interpret an outside image, or claim that administrative staff can decide clinical urgency.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clinical boundary review.","grader_hint":"Check the whole document for clinical conclusions beyond administrative triage and escalation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo states that routine missing-record handling must not delay escalation of a potentially urgent clinical concern.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Safety boundary.","grader_hint":"Require explicit priority over ordinary administrative workflow."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo includes a privacy-conscious approach to fax confirmations, tickets, and notes.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy language.","grader_hint":"Accept minimum-necessary handling, secure routing, or avoiding unnecessary patient details."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo requires identity or patient-record matching before outside material is treated as belonging to the appointment.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Record-integrity control.","grader_hint":"Look for at least two appropriate matching elements or equivalent verification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo calls for documenting the status, source, and date or time of record receipt or follow-up.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Documentation control.","grader_hint":"The documentation expectation must support auditability and handoff."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo assigns ownership for resolving a missing or unusable record rather than leaving responsibility diffuse.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ownership language.","grader_hint":"Accept a role-based owner with a clear handoff boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo explains how the selected change should work for both internal scheduling staff and external referring offices.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stakeholder-impact analysis.","grader_hint":"Require both sides of the intake pathway, not only internal staff."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo acknowledges that referring offices may have different causes or patterns of missing records.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Segmentation or limitation statement.","grader_hint":"A blanket claim that one cause affects every office should not receive credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo proposes monitoring for unintended effects such as unnecessary repeat requests, delayed care, or increased administrative burden.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk or monitoring discussion.","grader_hint":"At least one plausible unintended effect and a way to notice it must appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies a reasonable distinction between a transmission failure and a request or documentation failure.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cause taxonomy.","grader_hint":"The distinction should affect what evidence or control is relevant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo uses chronology or version/change timing to assess the spring reminder-template explanation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Temporal reasoning.","grader_hint":"Require more than naming the spring edit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo uses routing or delivery evidence to assess the changed-fax-number explanation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transmission reasoning.","grader_hint":"Look for confirmation or failure patterns tied to the destination number."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo uses content or request-language evidence to assess the missing-field and missing-report explanations.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Request-design reasoning.","grader_hint":"The analysis must connect what staff asked for to what was actually received."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo identifies a residual risk even after the recommended change is implemented.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Residual-risk statement.","grader_hint":"Accept external nonresponse, incorrect matching, or clinically urgent material as examples."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo states what the change cannot establish or guarantee, avoiding an absolute claim that missing records will be eliminated.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations or conclusion.","grader_hint":"Reject certainty that the fix will remove all missed records or all wasted visits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any numeric target or threshold introduced by the memo is labeled as a proposed management target or assumption rather than an observed fact.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Quantitative labels.","grader_hint":"A target is acceptable if its status and measurement basis are clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo's cause conclusion, projected effect, recommended change, owner, and success measure are mutually consistent.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document coherence.","grader_hint":"Trace the selected mechanism through the forecast and recommendation; deduct if sections point to different fixes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memo ends with one clear decision and does not end with an open-ended list of next steps or competing options.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph or decision statement.","grader_hint":"The closing should restate one call, with supporting actions framed as implementation details rather than additional decisions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A senior decision maker can understand the problem, the evidentiary basis, the uncertainty, the safety boundary, and the one proposed change without asking the author to decode the memo.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document as a whole.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity, hierarchy, concise explanation, and whether jargon or unexplained caveats make the briefing unusable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memo is professionally usable: concise, neutral, privacy-conscious, readable, and appropriately instructional for a healthcare operations briefing rather than a system manual.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document as a whole.","grader_hint":"Assess writing quality, visual legibility, tone, and whether the document supports a real executive decision."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an enterprise-facing healthcare administration briefing in which a medical secretary turns incomplete operational traces into a safe, decision-ready explanation for clinical operations leadership. The document must communicate facts and uncertainty without pretending to have evidence that was not supplied.","difficulty_sources":["Four plausible explanations must be compared using incomplete operational traces rather than selected by instruction.","The solver must project implications under assumptions without inventing unavailable data.","The informal promise must be reconciled with truthful record-status communication and clinical safety.","A rare, severe clinical-information exception must be separated from routine administrative guidance.","The final briefing must make one decision while remaining useful to an executive reader."],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Create a source-free authored-document task in which the solver must produce an instructional executive briefing memo from the scenario and occupational knowledge, without being handed a causal answer or a local policy.","solver_feasible_content":{"setting":"Cravenhurst ENT & Audiology; Marta Kilbride prepares the memo for Yusra Behrmann before the fifth provider is added in October.","available_facts":["Approximately one in five new patients arrives without outside imaging or an audiogram needed for the visit.","The appointment may be burned at check-in.","Available traces are fax confirmations, Zendesk tickets, and scheduling notes.","Four plausible explanations are the missing records field on the referral fax form, the script requesting imaging but not the report, the spring reminder-template edit, and the changed intake fax number.","Yusra has made an informal promise to a high-volume referring office not to reject or delay its referrals for missing records.","A rare severe case involving potentially urgent findings in outside material must be treated separately from routine missing-record handling."],"professional_expectations":["Separate observed facts, assumptions, projections, and unknowns.","Use chronology, recurrence, corroboration, source coverage, and routing/content evidence to compare competing causes.","Select one leading cause or one explicitly bounded causal combination and explain uncertainty.","Recommend one operational change with owner, timing, control point, and measurable success indicator.","Define record completeness beyond mere transmission; distinguish the image from the associated report.","Protect patient identity and preserve record matching, receipt/status documentation, and auditability.","Create a separate escalation path for possible urgent clinical concerns and do not allow administrative staff to diagnose or determine clinical urgency.","Preserve service to the referring office without falsely representing incomplete material as clinically ready.","Use a conditional scenario or assumption-based projection because no clean underlying dataset is supplied.","End on one decision, not a menu of options or an open-ended next-step list."]},"gold_logic":{"expected_shape":"A polished, no-more-than-three-page executive briefing memo with a concise problem statement, evidence inventory, comparative hypothesis analysis, one leading explanation, conditional October implication, one recommended change, safety/relationship boundaries, ownership, timing, and measurement.","causal_reasoning":"The gold should not require one invented literal answer. It should reward a defensible choice based on the four supplied hypotheses and should accept a bounded combination only when the document explains why the evidence cannot isolate one mechanism. The explanation must use later or repeated traces, distinguish transmission from request-content failures, and state confirmatory and disconfirming evidence.","projection_logic":"Because no source dataset is provided, the gold must accept qualitative or formula-based projections under explicit assumptions. It must reject invented measured counts, dollars, patient facts, or precise causal percentages presented as observed.","recommendation_logic":"Exactly one primary change is required. Supporting safeguards, communications, ownership, and monitoring are implementation details, not separate recommendations. The recommendation must address the chosen mechanism and include an accountable owner, timing before October, a pre-visit or equivalent control point, and a measurable before/after indicator.","tension_logic":"The informal promise must be acknowledged but cannot override truthful communication, record integrity, or clinical safety. The rare potentially urgent-finding case must have a distinct prompt clinical escalation path and cannot be handled as ordinary missing paperwork.","quality_logic":"The document should be understandable to Yusra without author clarification, avoid system click-through instructions, remain neutral and privacy-conscious, and close with one clear decision."},"constraints_not_to_seed_in_reference":["Do not create hidden patient-level records or numerical observations that the solver could not know.","Do not require a private code, exact local form field, exact fax number, or invented legal citation.","Do not force a particular one of the four causes as the only acceptable answer; score reasoning and defensibility.","Do not specify page layout, table count, font, row count, or section order."],"gold_build_method":"Have an independent domain reviewer draft a plausible memo using only the request and general healthcare administrative practice, then audit it against all 60 predicates. Revise the rubric only where a criterion is not independently checkable from the submitted DOCX. Render the resulting document offline to verify the page cap and readability."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"root_cause_briefing_memo","filename":"cravenhurst_records_root_cause_briefing.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A concise executive root-cause briefing memo explaining the missing outside-records problem, weighing competing explanations, projecting implications before October, and making one defensible recommended change.","required":true,"constraints":["The file must be written for Yusra Behrmann as an executive or senior decision maker.","The memo must be no more than three pages.","The memo must make exactly one recommendation and end with that decision.","Observed facts, assumptions, projections, and unknowns must be distinguishable.","Do not include patient identifiers, invented patient-level facts, invented measured rates, unsupported legal claims, or system click instructions."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"document_information","work_product":"customer_case_response","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"medical_secretaries_admin_assistants","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":2.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use one healthcare-administration domain reviewer for substantive safety, workflow, and evidentiary judgment, plus one independent reviewer for reachability, coherence, page-count, and artifact usability. Resolve disagreements by requiring the submitted memo itself to show the evidence or reasoning.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No actual fax, ticket, or scheduling-note dataset is supplied, so causal selection and October projection must be judged for defensibility and explicit uncertainty rather than against a hidden numeric answer.","Some valid recommendations may use different operational mechanisms; the rubric therefore evaluates causal fit, safety, ownership, and measurement rather than one mandated intervention."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T21:04:42+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T21:04:42+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T21:04:42+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T21:04:42+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-24T21:04:42+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c2/runs/08d17d9ce0e9eb69","declared_deliverables":["cravenhurst_records_root_cause_briefing.docx"]};
